		<h2>Emissions factor derivation</h2>
		<p>
			The result of applying the UNFCCC IFI-TWG GHG Accounting Protocol to the ISO-NE grid is
			approximately 775 lbs CO2e/MWh. The Combined Margin is 75% Operating Margin plus 25% Build
			Margin, where the Operating Margin is the GHG emissions value of the top 50% of highest
			priced, last dispatched grid resources. On the New England grid, these are natural gas and
			oil powered plants. The Build Margin is comprised of the average emission factors for new
			generating resources expected to come online over the next 8 years. In New England, these
			resources will be almost entirely renewables and battery storage. I have assigned a 100 lbs
			CO2/MWh value to these Build resources. These emissions are largely comprised of cradle to
			grave, embodied energy related emissions. Therefore
		</p>
		<p>
			Combined Margin = (0.75 &times; 1,000) + (0.25 &times; 100) = 775 lbs CO2e/MWh
		</p>
		<p>
			The analysis of lifetime (25 year) solar energy production from 1 acre of solar PV results
			in 8,374 MWh. This value is net of (1) annual solar output decline over years 1 to 25 due to
			panel efficiency losses, resulting in 87.5% efficiency at year 25; (2) 4.5% distribution and
			transmission losses; and (3) an assumed 5% total lifetime curtailment.
		</p>
		<p>8,374 MWh &times; 775 lbs CO2e/MWh = 2,948 MT CO2e per lifetime</p>
		<p>
			From this I subtract 655 MT CO2e to account for one-time emissions related to solar
			development. First, 132 MT CO2e/acre for the carbon emissions associated with the embodied
			energy in solar and BESS production and transportation. Second, 523 MT CO2e/acre for
			one-time forest emissions (300 MT CO2e/acre) and foregone forest sequestration for 25 years
			(50 MT CO2e/acre), with a 1.67 multiplier applied to each of the forest carbon loss factors
			to account for the additional land required beyond the solar PV footprint, (300 + 50)
			&times; 1.67 = 583. Finally, I add back 60 MT CO2e/acre for average New England carbon
			recovery (20%) during tree harvesting in the form of long-term carbon storage in furniture,
			flooring and lumber.
		</p>
		<p>Lifetime net adjustments to 1 acre of solar CO2e mitigation benefits are (-132) + (-583) + 60 = -655 MT CO2e.</p>
		<p>
			2,948 MT CO2e per solar acre of 25-year displacement, less the net adjustment of 655 MT
			CO2e, gives a 25-year mitigation of 2,293 MT CO2e per solar acre. This represents a -22%
			adjustment from the original calculation, since 2,948 - 2,293 = 655 and 655 / 2,948 = 22%.
			For the purposes of adjusting the emissions factor per MWh applied in the presentation, I
			took the UNFCCC/ISO-NE derived factor of 775 lbs CO2e/MWh and reduced it by 22% to 600 lbs
			CO2e/MWh.
		</p>

